777:
It shall be unlawful for any person, individual or corporation, its agents or employees to do or allow any of the following acts in the county parks:
(1) Bring any domesticated animal into any public building;
(2) Allow any domesticated animal to threaten the safety, security, peace of mind or enjoyment of others;
(3) Fail to restrain any domesticated animal with a leash or other instrument of six (6) feet or less in length;
(4) Leave any domesticated animal unattended;
(5) Allow a domesticated animal to enter or come within fifty (50) feet of concession or seating areas during and thirty (30) minutes prior to or after a sporting event;
(6) Allow any domesticated animal to enter upon the athletic fields;
(7) Fail to pick up or remove and properly dispose of outside the park of excrement of any domesticated animal;
(8) Allow any domesticated hoofed animal such as a horse, pig, donkey or cattle to enter upon the premises without express written permission of the county; or
(9) Allow any domesticated animal, such as a dog, cat or other domesticated pet, to enter Village Park at North Henry located at 750 Fairview Road in Ellenwood, Georgia, without express written permission of the county.
(Ord. No. 91-04, 7-16-91; Ord. No. 17-05, 4-4-17)

781:
(a) Title. This ordinance shall be known as the "Henry County Sponsorship and Naming Rights Ordinance."
(b) Authority. This ordinance is adopted pursuant to the home rule powers granted to counties under the Georgia Constitution, Article IX, Section II, Paragraph I, and applicable provisions of O.C.G.A. § 36-1-20 et seq., and the county's authority to manage and control its property, contracts, and fiscal affairs.
(c) Purpose and intent. The purpose of this ordinance is to:
1. Establish a uniform policy governing sponsorships and naming rights for county-owned or controlled facilities, properties, programs, and services;
2. Generate revenue and offset operational or capital costs;
3. Enhance public services through strategic partnerships;
4. Protect the county from undue influence, commercialization, or reputational harm;
5. Ensure all sponsorship arrangements are transparent, competitively procured where required, and consistent with the public interest.
(d) Definitions. For purposes of this ordinance:
• "Sponsorship" means a financial or in-kind contribution provided in exchange for recognition, marketing benefits, or limited association with a county asset.
• "Naming Rights" means the contractual right to associate a corporate or organizational name with a county facility, property, or program for a defined period.
• "County Asset" includes any county-owned or operated building, park, facility, infrastructure, program, or service.
• "Unsolicited Proposal" means a sponsorship or naming rights proposal initiated by a private entity without a formal county solicitation.
(e) General policy.
1. The county may enter into sponsorship and naming rights agreements consistent with this ordinance.
2. The county shall at all times:
a. Retain ownership and full control over all county assets;
b. Preserve the public purpose, function, and accessibility of all county assets;
c. Avoid any arrangement that creates actual or perceived endorsement of a sponsor's products, services, or viewpoints.
3. Nothing in this ordinance shall obligate the county to enter into any sponsorship agreement.
(f) Solicited sponsorships and naming rights.
1. The county may identify sponsorship or naming rights opportunities and solicit proposals through a competitive procurement process.
2. Procurement requirements.
a. All solicited opportunities shall comply with:
o The Henry County Purchasing Ordinance;
o Applicable provisions of O.C.G.A. Title 36;
o Any applicable state or federal procurement requirements.
b. The county may utilize Requests for Proposals (RFPs), Invitations to Bid (ITBs), or other competitive processes.
3. Minimum requirements for agreements. All agreements shall:
a. Be for a fixed term and include a defined expiration date;
b. Contain a non-automatic renewal provision requiring board approval;
c. Include a termination for convenience clause;
d. Include a termination clause to protect the county's reputation;
e. Specify all financial and/or in-kind consideration;
f. Require compliance with all applicable laws and county policies;
g. Prohibit assignment or transfer without county approval;
h. Require removal or modification of signage upon termination.
4. All agreements are subject to approval by the Henry County Board of Commissioners.
(g) Unsolicited sponsorship proposals.
1. The county may consider unsolicited proposals.
2. Submission requirements. Proposals must include:
a. Description of the proposed sponsorship or naming rights;
b. Identification of the county asset involved;
c. Proposed term and renewal options;
d. Financial or in-kind consideration and valuation methodology;
e. Payment schedule;
f. Proposed rights and benefits;
g. Any requested exclusivity provisions.
3. Review process.
a. The county manager shall establish a review committee.
b. The committee shall evaluate:
o Public benefit;
o Financial value;
o Operational impact;
o Legal and reputational risk.
c. The county may, at its discretion, require a competitive process before proceeding.
4. Final approval rests with the board of commissioners.
(h) Naming rights policy.
1. Naming rights may be granted for:
a. Buildings;
b. Parks and recreational facilities;
c. Rooms, fields, or components within facilities;
d. Programs or events.
2. Naming rights shall:
a. Be for a limited duration;
b. Not permanently rename county assets unless separately authorized;
c. Be subject to removal or change upon expiration or termination.
3. The county reserves the right to reject names that:
a. Are inconsistent with community standards;
b. Create confusion with existing public assets;
c. Imply county endorsement or political affiliation.
(i) Restrictions. The county shall not enter into sponsorship or naming rights agreements that involve:
1. Political candidates, campaigns, or partisan organizations;
2. Obscene, sexually explicit, or adult-oriented materials;
3. Illegal products or services;
4. Tobacco, vaping, or controlled substances;
5. Firearms or weapons-related marketing inconsistent with county policy;
6. Content that is discriminatory, defamatory, or promotes violence;
7. Any entity or message that could reasonably bring disrepute to the county.
(j) Financial management.
1. All revenues derived from sponsorships shall be:
a. Deposited into appropriate county accounts; and
b. Used for:
o Maintenance;
o Operations;
o Improvements; or
o Expansion of the associated asset or program, unless otherwise directed by the board.
2. In-kind contributions must be reasonably valued and documented.
(k) County rights and disclaimers.
1. The county:
a. Does not endorse sponsors or their products;
b. Retains full operational control over all assets;
c. May terminate agreements if necessary to protect public interest.
2. Agreements shall include indemnification provisions protecting the county.
3. No agreement shall create:
a. A partnership or joint venture; or
b. Any obligation for the county to purchase sponsor goods or services.
(l) Administration.
1. The county manager or designee shall:
a. Administer this ordinance;
b. Develop implementing policies and procedures;
c. Coordinate review and negotiation of agreements.
2. The county attorney shall review all agreements for legal sufficiency.
(m) Relation to other policies. This ordinance does not govern:
1. Traditional naming or renaming of county property;
2. Dedications or memorial namings;
3. Donations governed under separate county policies.
Said matters shall be governed by applicable county ordinances and policies.
(Ord. No. 26-02, 4-14-26)

